              ":question/id": "e4a94ca8-82ee-5776-a46d-b735d4ab60fb",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-371",
              ":question/problem": "Find the integral of $x^2 + 1.$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2+1}}{2+1}\\right)+x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "0db9b1c3-1e31-53f6-9fc2-1c611e2f9398",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Computing an Integral Using the Sum Rule"
          },
          {
